1
00:00:12,094 --> 00:00:15,556
Two drops in each ear twice a day.
We're gonna fix you right up, kid.

2
00:00:18,935 --> 00:00:20,061
Shouldn't Lisbon
have called by now?

3
00:00:22,313 --> 00:00:23,356
She said she would call
when there was a break in the case.

4
00:00:23,481 --> 00:00:25,233
- I mean, maybe there wasn't one.
- I'm gonna call her anyway.

5
00:00:25,733 --> 00:00:26,859
- You're such a worrier.
- Can you take her?

6
00:00:27,193 --> 00:00:28,236
- Yeah.
- No, I'm not!

7
00:00:28,819 --> 00:00:30,446
Except for when there's things
to worry about.

8
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:34,408
- "Hello?"
- Hey, it's Rigsby. Any news?

9
00:00:34,534 --> 00:00:36,077
I was gonna call you
from the car.

10
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:37,828
SFPD is sending
the case over to us.

11
00:00:38,329 --> 00:00:40,581
- Hey, they got the case from SFPD.
- Oh, that's great.

12
00:00:40,831 --> 00:00:41,874
That's great.
How'd you convince them?

13
00:00:41,999 --> 00:00:43,918
When you're FBI, people
tend to give you what you want.

14
00:00:45,336 --> 00:00:47,463
- Had they developed any leads?
- Not really.

15
00:00:47,588 --> 00:00:51,050
They agree that whoever murdered
Laroche and Ardiles Bugged our phones.

16
00:00:51,551 --> 00:00:54,470
It's probably somebody we arrested
at the CBI someone holding a grudge.

17
00:00:54,637 --> 00:00:56,514
I'm putting together a list.
I'll keep you posted.

18
00:00:56,681 --> 00:00:58,099
Okay. That's great.
I'll talk to you soon.

19
00:01:02,228 --> 00:01:03,938
- Wayne?
- Coming.

20
00:01:08,484 --> 00:01:10,945
Can you give her drops
while I get her bath started?

21
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:12,405
Oh, sure. Yeah.
Thanks.

22
00:01:13,531 --> 00:01:14,574
Go see daddy.

23
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:16,742
Go see daddy.

24
00:01:17,994 --> 00:01:19,036
Come on.

25
00:01:19,453 --> 00:01:20,496
There we go.

26
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:23,583
Okay.

27
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:27,211
All righty.
Let's see what we got.

28
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:39,557
Grace?

29
00:01:47,815 --> 00:01:48,858
All righty, Kiddo.

30
00:01:50,318 --> 00:01:52,278
This won't hurt a bit.

31
00:01:52,778 --> 00:01:54,322
At least I think it won't.

32
00:01:56,616 --> 00:01:57,658
No!

33
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:44,955
Wayne!
Are you all right?!

34
00:02:45,498 --> 00:02:47,875
Wayne, is she hurt?
Is she hurt?

35
00:02:48,292 --> 00:02:49,835
She's okay. She's okay.

36
00:02:50,002 --> 00:02:51,379
- Oh, my God. Oh, my God.
- She's okay.

37
00:02:52,171 --> 00:02:53,214
We're okay.

38
00:03:10,354 --> 00:03:13,440
And if she has trouble falling asleep
try taking her for a ride in the car.

39
00:03:13,691 --> 00:03:14,733
That usually puts her right out.

40
00:03:16,402 --> 00:03:18,070
Thanks. I'll call you later.

41
00:03:19,446 --> 00:03:20,489
Maddy's gonna be fine
with your aunt.

42
00:03:21,115 --> 00:03:22,157
I know.

43
00:03:22,449 --> 00:03:23,992
And Ben's gonna be fine
with his mother.

44
00:03:24,159 --> 00:03:25,661
{\an8}- They're both gonna be just fine.
- Sure.

45
00:03:26,370 --> 00:03:27,413
{\an8}Hey, come on.

46
00:03:29,164 --> 00:03:30,207
Hey.

47
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:32,793
{\an8}Welcome to Austin.

48
00:03:33,669 --> 00:03:34,920
{\an8}- You okay?
- Yeah.

49
00:03:35,629 --> 00:03:37,673
- Yeah.
- We're okay, considering.

50
00:03:37,965 --> 00:03:40,050
- You settled into your motel?
- Yeah.

51
00:03:40,843 --> 00:03:43,345
{\an8}Well, we heard back from the crime techs
in San Francisco.

52
00:03:43,554 --> 00:03:44,596
It looks like the intruder

53
00:03:44,722 --> 00:03:46,306
got into your house
through a basement window

54
00:03:46,432 --> 00:03:47,683
{\an8}that wasn't on your alarm system.

55
00:03:48,434 --> 00:03:49,643
{\an8}Did forensics find anything?

56
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:51,395
{\an8}No, ballistics ran the casings

57
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:52,771
{\an8}that were recovered
from the crime scene.

58
00:03:52,980 --> 00:03:55,023
{\an8}- There weren't any matches.
- Any eyewitnesses?

59
00:03:55,315 --> 00:03:57,443
{\an8}People heard shots,
but nobody saw anything.

60
00:03:58,152 --> 00:03:59,653
Come on. We're just about
to start the briefing.

61
00:04:01,029 --> 00:04:04,032
{\an8}Hey. We're gonna get this guy.
Come on.

62
00:04:05,701 --> 00:04:07,202
{\an8}We believe the assailant
is the same person

63
00:04:07,327 --> 00:04:08,829
that killed attorney Osvaldo Ardiles

64
00:04:09,538 --> 00:04:12,833
and former California Bureau
of Investigation agent J.J. Laroche.

65
00:04:15,169 --> 00:04:18,255
{\an8}Now, in your packets you'll find
a preliminary list of suspects.

66
00:04:18,672 --> 00:04:19,715
{\an8}Some are still incarcerated

67
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:21,633
{\an8}but it's possible he's orchestrating
this from prison.

68
00:04:22,009 --> 00:04:24,678
{\an8}Now, there are 12 names,
so we have our work cut out for us.

69
00:04:25,304 --> 00:04:26,597
{\an8}Acquaint yourselves
with the case files.

70
00:04:27,055 --> 00:04:29,516
{\an8}I'll be assigning individual suspects
for you to investigate

71
00:04:29,683 --> 00:04:30,809
{\an8}later this morning.
Thank you.

72
00:04:32,978 --> 00:04:34,646
{\an8}- Good to see you, guys.
- Yeah, you, too.

73
00:04:35,314 --> 00:04:36,356
{\an8}Hey.

74
00:04:37,983 --> 00:04:39,026
{\an8}- How you doing?
- All right.

75
00:04:41,028 --> 00:04:42,738
Just got word that the FBI
has officially hired you

76
00:04:42,863 --> 00:04:43,906
as consultants for the case.

77
00:04:44,156 --> 00:04:46,283
You're the ones who first found out
we were being tracked.

78
00:04:46,408 --> 00:04:47,951
You know this case
better than anybody.

79
00:04:48,202 --> 00:04:49,828
Don't worry.
We'll figure out who it is.

80
00:04:49,995 --> 00:04:52,039
Not anytime soon,
the way you're going about this.

81
00:04:52,498 --> 00:04:53,540
12 suspects?

82
00:04:53,665 --> 00:04:55,667
I had Wylie run the suspects
through the Vicap database.

83
00:04:56,376 --> 00:04:57,503
Those are the ones
that were flagged.

84
00:04:57,669 --> 00:04:58,712
What's their connection
to Ardiles?

85
00:04:58,879 --> 00:05:00,214
Well, he was involved
in all their prosecutions.

86
00:05:00,714 --> 00:05:03,091
{\an8}Well, that's fine and Dandy,
but we're the ones that caught them

87
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:04,343
and questioned them

88
00:05:04,468 --> 00:05:06,136
and we certainly know them
better than any database.

89
00:05:06,637 --> 00:05:09,014
- What do you think we should do?
- I think we should trust our instincts.

90
00:05:09,181 --> 00:05:11,517
{\an8}- You mean take a wild guess.
- 80% of the time

91
00:05:11,642 --> 00:05:14,186
{\an8}a detective's first guess is right.

92
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:15,562
You just made that up.

93
00:05:15,687 --> 00:05:17,856
And you knew that
because your instincts told you.

94
00:05:18,065 --> 00:05:19,733
Now, come on.
Everyone, pick a photo.

95
00:05:20,442 --> 00:05:23,904
{\an8}- Whoever looks right.
- John Hutten, the bank robber.

96
00:05:24,530 --> 00:05:26,198
{\an8}He's smart, determined.

97
00:05:26,615 --> 00:05:27,825
I could see him
trying something like this.

98
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:29,409
Yeah, you have a hunch.
Grace, don't be shy.

99
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:30,869
I'll go with Donny Culpepper.

100
00:05:31,119 --> 00:05:33,455
He's a professional criminal
implicated in several murders.

101
00:05:33,789 --> 00:05:34,832
This wouldn't be a stretch.

102
00:05:35,249 --> 00:05:37,835
- Cho, you know you want to.
- Tommy Volker.

103
00:05:38,126 --> 00:05:40,712
A sociopath if ever there was one.
Lisbon?

104
00:05:41,338 --> 00:05:42,506
I don't know. Richard Haibach?

105
00:05:43,382 --> 00:05:44,508
Linus Wagner, maybe.

106
00:05:44,883 --> 00:05:47,594
The pedophile
and the homicidal shrink.

107
00:05:47,719 --> 00:05:48,762
Excellent choices.

108
00:05:49,429 --> 00:05:51,098
Now we have
a set of suspects.

109
00:05:51,473 --> 00:05:52,891
{\an8}Okay, we'll go with those five.

110
00:05:53,058 --> 00:05:54,351
{\an8}I'll have my agents
work the other seven.

111
00:05:54,643 --> 00:05:56,103
{\an8}To start, we need to know
who's in prison

112
00:05:56,395 --> 00:05:58,272
{\an8}who's out, and who
they've talked to recently.

113
00:05:58,438 --> 00:06:00,315
Yes, well, that's not really
my sort of thing

114
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:02,067
{\an8}so if you need any more help,
I'll be on my couch.

115
00:06:02,192 --> 00:06:03,235
{\an8}I need some help.

116
00:06:03,694 --> 00:06:05,487
{\an8}- Hi. Agent Fischer.
- Oh, hey.

117
00:06:06,488 --> 00:06:09,867
I want you to know that everyone here
is 100% behind this investigation.

118
00:06:10,158 --> 00:06:11,243
- We appreciate that.
- Thank you.

119
00:06:14,454 --> 00:06:17,207
But since there's nothing for you
to do here I'm stealing you.

120
00:06:17,374 --> 00:06:18,417
We've got another case.

121
00:06:19,001 --> 00:06:20,043
No rest for the wicked.

122
00:06:20,168 --> 00:06:21,545
Where did the wheel of death
stop today?

123
00:06:21,753 --> 00:06:23,755
Bradley, Texas, an hour west.

124
00:06:24,172 --> 00:06:26,174
A body was found this morning
on a fracking site.

125
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:48,572
"A worker discovered the body
in a wastewater pit."

126
00:06:49,072 --> 00:06:51,617
"The victim's name is Dan Becker.
He's a local rancher..."

127
00:06:51,742 --> 00:06:53,452
{\an8}"Small-time, 50 acres or so."

128
00:06:53,827 --> 00:06:54,953
{\an8}"Couple of months ago,
he brought a lawsuit"

129
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:57,456
{\an8}against a regional energy
company called Millman oil.

130
00:06:58,165 --> 00:06:59,207
{\an8}They're the Frackers?

131
00:06:59,416 --> 00:07:02,044
{\an8}Yeah, they've been developing frack sites
for the past couple of years.

132
00:07:02,502 --> 00:07:04,755
{\an8}The one where the body was found
is leased federal land.

133
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:05,923
{\an8}That's why we caught the case.

134
00:07:06,506 --> 00:07:07,758
{\an8}Becker sued because he claimed

135
00:07:07,966 --> 00:07:09,968
{\an8}the company polluted
his ranch's groundwater.

136
00:07:10,135 --> 00:07:13,680
{\an8}An anti-Fracker found dead on the site
of the company he just sued.

137
00:07:13,805 --> 00:07:14,848
That's murder gold.

138
00:07:14,973 --> 00:07:16,850
{\an8}Well, we don't know
the company's involved.

139
00:07:16,975 --> 00:07:18,477
{\an8}We shouldn't leap
to any conclusions.

140
00:07:18,644 --> 00:07:19,686
Of course we should.

141
00:07:20,646 --> 00:07:21,939
{\an8}That way, it'll go a little quicker.

142
00:07:40,791 --> 00:07:42,793
Sheriff says the coroner
took the body about an hour ago.

143
00:07:43,168 --> 00:07:44,711
Oh, good.
Seen enough of those.

144
00:07:46,213 --> 00:07:48,006
Excuse me, gentlemen.
Hi, there.

145
00:07:48,757 --> 00:07:51,051
I'm FBI agent Fischer.
This is my colleague, Patrick Jane.

146
00:07:51,218 --> 00:07:53,553
We'd like to ask you a few questions
about Dan Becker's murder.

147
00:07:53,804 --> 00:07:55,889
Just... just you.
The rest of you can go home.

148
00:07:56,014 --> 00:07:57,057
- No, they can't.
- Yes, they can.

149
00:07:57,182 --> 00:07:58,392
They just work here.
He's in charge.

150
00:07:58,934 --> 00:08:00,477
Well, stay close.
We'll get to you in a minute.

151
00:08:00,644 --> 00:08:02,980
- What makes you say I'm in charge?
- Your snakeskin boots

152
00:08:04,147 --> 00:08:05,565
your nice, stiff black jeans.

153
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:07,442
Shiny belt buckle...

154
00:08:08,235 --> 00:08:10,320
That air of command
that you have about you.

155
00:08:12,072 --> 00:08:13,991
Well, I'm Jason Kern,
chief operating officer.

156
00:08:15,075 --> 00:08:16,910
Well, you make the tough
decisions around here.

157
00:08:17,035 --> 00:08:18,328
Who to fire, who to hire,
who to bribe.

158
00:08:19,955 --> 00:08:20,998
Well, I wish I could do that.

159
00:08:21,123 --> 00:08:22,749
Boy, that'd make my job easier,
wouldn't it?

160
00:08:22,874 --> 00:08:23,917
No need to be so coy.

161
00:08:24,501 --> 00:08:26,253
You're big oil.
You can do whatever you want, right?

162
00:08:28,046 --> 00:08:29,339
Well, you're a character, aren't you?

163
00:08:30,924 --> 00:08:32,009
So, why do you think
this was murder?

164
00:08:32,134 --> 00:08:34,553
The coroner says the likely cause of death
was a blow to the head.

165
00:08:34,761 --> 00:08:38,223
So it's murder, or he, uh...
fell and hit his head.

166
00:08:38,682 --> 00:08:41,727
Be that as it may, we'll assist
your investigation any way we can.

167
00:08:42,769 --> 00:08:46,314
Because we are not big oil, sir.
We're a regional company.

168
00:08:46,481 --> 00:08:48,817
We're part of the communities
we work in.

169
00:08:48,942 --> 00:08:50,402
When we succeed, they thrive.

170
00:08:52,237 --> 00:08:54,156
Something is not thriving.

171
00:08:54,364 --> 00:08:55,907
Yeah, our equipment
keeps shutting down.

172
00:08:56,742 --> 00:09:00,746
Started right after those yahoos
protested at our offices yesterday.

173
00:09:01,204 --> 00:09:02,664
What yahoos would that be?

174
00:09:03,123 --> 00:09:04,791
Group that calls itself
the cooperative.

175
00:09:05,125 --> 00:09:07,252
They're anti-corporate
"hacktivists"

176
00:09:07,419 --> 00:09:09,296
who spread mischief
over the internet.

177
00:09:09,629 --> 00:09:11,673
We think they're interfering
with the computer

178
00:09:11,798 --> 00:09:12,841
that runs
the hydraulic pumps.

179
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:15,844
Maybe you should be talking
to them about Becker.

180
00:09:16,011 --> 00:09:18,055
You think
an anti-corporate group

181
00:09:18,180 --> 00:09:20,348
was responsible for the murder
of an anti-Fracker?

182
00:09:20,599 --> 00:09:23,018
Ma'am, all I know is the cooperative
likes a grand gesture

183
00:09:23,351 --> 00:09:26,188
and they don't care who they hurt
to stop the corporate agenda.

184
00:09:26,730 --> 00:09:28,732
Now, would they kill a man
and try to blame us for it?

185
00:09:29,858 --> 00:09:30,901
I wouldn't put it past them.

186
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:35,906
Look, I'm afraid I got my hands
full just now, all right?

187
00:09:36,323 --> 00:09:38,241
You have more questions,
you all come by the office.

188
00:09:38,366 --> 00:09:39,576
It'll be easier to chat there.

189
00:09:40,077 --> 00:09:41,453
All right, guys.
What's... what's going on here?

190
00:09:41,787 --> 00:09:42,829
Folksy.

191
00:09:44,998 --> 00:09:47,501
Yeah, let's go talk to Becker's family
see what they have to say.

192
00:09:48,168 --> 00:09:49,419
Okeydokey, ma'am.

193
00:09:53,423 --> 00:09:54,883
Well, that's one suspect
eliminated.

194
00:09:55,008 --> 00:09:56,593
Linus Wagner's been
in solitary confinement

195
00:09:56,718 --> 00:09:57,761
for the past five months.

196
00:09:58,011 --> 00:09:59,596
You can take Culpepper
off the list.

197
00:09:59,721 --> 00:10:01,264
He got married
and moved to Venezuela.

198
00:10:01,389 --> 00:10:02,891
He hasn't been back in the states
in over a year.

199
00:10:04,017 --> 00:10:05,060
So who do you like now?

200
00:10:05,310 --> 00:10:06,812
I keep on coming back
to Haibach.

201
00:10:07,187 --> 00:10:08,980
He was on Jane's fake Red John list.

202
00:10:09,356 --> 00:10:11,566
Kirkland tortured and nearly killed him.
I'd hold a grudge.

203
00:10:11,733 --> 00:10:12,776
Does he have an alibi?

204
00:10:12,901 --> 00:10:14,152
I have a call
Into his office in Denver.

205
00:10:14,778 --> 00:10:15,821
I got something.

206
00:10:16,029 --> 00:10:17,072
John Hutten's out of prison.

207
00:10:17,239 --> 00:10:19,574
Apparently he's on work release.
His new address is in Houston.

208
00:10:19,783 --> 00:10:20,951
That's two hours from here.

209
00:10:21,076 --> 00:10:22,786
But Hutten's a bank robber.
He's not a killer.

210
00:10:22,911 --> 00:10:25,080
Well, whoever attacked the house
bypassed the security system

211
00:10:25,205 --> 00:10:26,414
- and that took planning.
- That sounds like Hutten.

212
00:10:27,332 --> 00:10:28,959
I'll go talk to him. Sure.
- Mind if I come with?

213
00:10:29,376 --> 00:10:30,418
- Can I drive?
- No.

214
00:10:30,544 --> 00:10:31,586
Why not?

215
00:10:31,711 --> 00:10:32,754
Consultants don't drive.
It's policy.

216
00:10:32,879 --> 00:10:33,922
- Whose?
- Mine.

217
00:10:34,047 --> 00:10:35,090
Oh, come on.

218
00:10:41,555 --> 00:10:42,722
- Mrs. Becker?
- No.

219
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:45,725
I'm... I'm Susan...
Susan Fitzgerald.

220
00:10:45,851 --> 00:10:49,396
I'm a family friend.
But Molly's here somewhere.

221
00:10:49,604 --> 00:10:52,440
I'm agent Fischer with the FBI.
This is Patrick Jane.

222
00:10:52,858 --> 00:10:55,569
- May we come in?
- Of course, yes.

223
00:11:01,867 --> 00:11:02,909
This is my husband, Emmett.

224
00:11:03,827 --> 00:11:05,495
- Hello.
- Hi.

225
00:11:06,371 --> 00:11:08,456
I'm... I'm sorry.
Where is the kitchen?

226
00:11:09,166 --> 00:11:10,959
- It's right through there.
- Thank you.

227
00:11:12,002 --> 00:11:13,295
They're with the FBI.

228
00:11:15,714 --> 00:11:18,383
- How long did you two know Dan?
- Oh, since he was a kid.

229
00:11:18,717 --> 00:11:19,759
We've always been close.

230
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:21,887
That's what
makes this so hard.

231
00:11:25,223 --> 00:11:27,309
- Who are you?
- I'm Patrick Jane.

232
00:11:27,851 --> 00:11:30,228
I'm with the FBI.
You... you must be Molly.

233
00:11:30,437 --> 00:11:31,479
Sorry for your loss.

234
00:11:33,148 --> 00:11:34,191
Thank you.

235
00:11:35,442 --> 00:11:37,527
I wouldn't fill that in the tap.
Use the bottled water.

236
00:11:38,695 --> 00:11:41,198
Groundwater's full of methane.
Been that way almost a year.

237
00:11:41,489 --> 00:11:42,741
- Fracking.
- Yeah.

238
00:11:42,949 --> 00:11:43,992
Wonderful, isn't it?

239
00:11:44,326 --> 00:11:45,702
Killed off a bunch of our cattle, too.

240
00:11:46,119 --> 00:11:47,662
Well, I can see
why Dan wanted to sue.

241
00:11:48,288 --> 00:11:50,457
Dan was gonna win that lawsuit,
come hell or high water.

242
00:11:50,874 --> 00:11:52,125
You thought the suit
was a bad idea?

243
00:11:52,292 --> 00:11:53,501
I just didn't see the point.

244
00:11:54,085 --> 00:11:55,754
Nobody's gonna testify
against Millman oil.

245
00:11:55,879 --> 00:11:56,922
They make everybody
too much money.

246
00:11:57,964 --> 00:12:00,217
And that lawsuit did not make
Dan very popular in town

247
00:12:00,342 --> 00:12:02,052
- I'll tell you that much.
- There were other reasons, though.

248
00:12:02,677 --> 00:12:05,597
- Weren't there?
- We could've sold instead of suing.

249
00:12:06,556 --> 00:12:07,599
We need the money.

250
00:12:07,724 --> 00:12:09,392
And Dan could barely work
with his hurt back.

251
00:12:10,894 --> 00:12:13,063
And then Dan couldn't pass the ranch
on to his son.

252
00:12:13,438 --> 00:12:14,481
Nathaniel.

253
00:12:14,856 --> 00:12:16,358
He's on his way home
from a camping trip.

254
00:12:18,443 --> 00:12:22,656
It meant a lot to Dan
that his son would get the land

255
00:12:22,864 --> 00:12:24,783
that he got
from his father, and...

256
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:27,702
he was stubborn.

257
00:12:27,869 --> 00:12:29,037
Now there's nothing
stopping you.

258
00:12:29,788 --> 00:12:30,830
You gonna sell?

259
00:12:43,176 --> 00:12:44,219
Just as soon as I can.

260
00:12:47,013 --> 00:12:48,556
And what do you think
about Millman oil?

261
00:12:48,723 --> 00:12:50,433
- Well...
- I'll say it.

262
00:12:51,184 --> 00:12:52,227
They saved this town.

263
00:12:52,394 --> 00:12:54,145
It was dying.
They brought it back to life.

264
00:12:55,021 --> 00:12:57,440
So you don't think they had
anything to do with Dan's death?

265
00:12:57,565 --> 00:13:00,068
- No! No. No way.
- You're a fool, Fitzgerald.

266
00:13:02,028 --> 00:13:03,071
Oh, man.

267
00:13:03,446 --> 00:13:05,573
- And who are you?
- Bryce Kendrick.

268
00:13:06,783 --> 00:13:08,451
Me and Danny known each other
since we was kids.

269
00:13:09,577 --> 00:13:10,620
I knew him better than anybody.

270
00:13:11,538 --> 00:13:13,415
And I wouldn't trust these two idiots.
Millman oil...

271
00:13:14,499 --> 00:13:16,376
- They're behind this.
- Come on, Bryce.

272
00:13:16,543 --> 00:13:19,963
That company is ruining this town.
Police, politicians...

273
00:13:20,088 --> 00:13:22,215
They ain't lifting a finger
because they're bought off.

274
00:13:22,674 --> 00:13:23,717
Here we go again.

275
00:13:23,842 --> 00:13:24,884
Nobody dares cross them.

276
00:13:26,886 --> 00:13:28,680
Dan tried. Hmm?
Look what happened to him.

277
00:13:28,805 --> 00:13:30,265
They didn't kill Dan.
That's crazy.

278
00:13:31,891 --> 00:13:33,727
Only reason
you're saying that, Emmett...

279
00:13:34,602 --> 00:13:35,854
Is 'cause
you're in bed with them.

280
00:13:36,021 --> 00:13:37,897
Yeah, by the way,
they sold their fracking rights

281
00:13:38,023 --> 00:13:39,065
quick as they could.

282
00:13:39,983 --> 00:13:41,776
Money whores, the both of them.

283
00:13:41,943 --> 00:13:43,153
You would've done
the same thing

284
00:13:43,570 --> 00:13:44,821
if you had any land
worth selling.

285
00:13:45,155 --> 00:13:46,197
Yeah.

286
00:13:46,323 --> 00:13:47,615
- You smug son of a bitch.
- Hey!

287
00:13:51,119 --> 00:13:52,162
It's all right, everyone.

288
00:13:52,287 --> 00:13:53,330
- Yeah.
- It's under control.

289
00:13:54,998 --> 00:13:56,041
I'm cool. I'm cool.

290
00:14:00,045 --> 00:14:01,087
Sorry.

291
00:14:04,549 --> 00:14:06,926
Now, why are you so convinced
that Millman oil killed Dan?

292
00:14:07,218 --> 00:14:08,261
About a couple of weeks ago

293
00:14:08,386 --> 00:14:10,013
they sent a bunch of thugs out here
to rough him up

294
00:14:11,139 --> 00:14:12,766
Hurt him bad.
Busted up his back.

295
00:14:12,891 --> 00:14:14,351
They wanted old Danny
to drop his lawsuit.

296
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:17,187
- Said he wouldn't do it.
- I didn't see a police report.

297
00:14:17,354 --> 00:14:19,522
He didn't go to the police.
I told you...

298
00:14:20,690 --> 00:14:22,692
Everyone in this town
is on their payroll.

299
00:14:22,942 --> 00:14:25,820
- Did Dan know who ordered the attack?
- He had his suspicions, yeah.

300
00:14:26,738 --> 00:14:28,406
Yeah, there was someone
behind the payoffs

301
00:14:28,615 --> 00:14:29,657
and the threats and...

302
00:14:32,035 --> 00:14:33,536
some bigwig
with Millman oil.

303
00:14:34,079 --> 00:14:35,830
- Which bigwig?
- Danny didn't know.

304
00:14:37,707 --> 00:14:38,750
But it was one of them.

305
00:14:48,093 --> 00:14:49,135
- Hi.
- Hi.

306
00:14:49,886 --> 00:14:50,929
Can I help you?

307
00:14:51,054 --> 00:14:52,347
FBI, ma'am.
We're looking for John Hutten.

308
00:14:55,392 --> 00:14:57,477
Johnny?
Johnny, FBI's here.

309
00:14:58,478 --> 00:15:00,146
It's okay, darling.
Go ahead.

310
00:15:01,398 --> 00:15:02,440
Sorry I can't stay.

311
00:15:04,901 --> 00:15:07,529
- See you Wednesday afternoon, okay?
- Yeah. Have a good flight.

312
00:15:09,322 --> 00:15:10,907
I remember you guys
from California.

313
00:15:11,282 --> 00:15:12,992
FBI, huh?
That's a step up.

314
00:15:13,201 --> 00:15:15,453
- What are you doing out of prison?
- Spending time with my wife.

315
00:15:16,121 --> 00:15:18,540
She's a good woman.
I enjoy domestic life.

316
00:15:19,082 --> 00:15:20,125
How did you
get out so soon?

317
00:15:20,375 --> 00:15:22,293
Behaved myself.
They let me go.

318
00:15:23,002 --> 00:15:24,796
Prison overcrowding...
it's a problem.

319
00:15:25,004 --> 00:15:26,256
You been to San Francisco recently?

320
00:15:26,464 --> 00:15:27,841
- Why?
- Answer the question.

321
00:15:28,133 --> 00:15:29,217
I don't know that I remember.

322
00:15:29,384 --> 00:15:30,760
Maybe it'll come to you
back at our office.

323
00:15:30,969 --> 00:15:32,679
- Let's go.
- That's an excellent idea.

324
00:15:35,432 --> 00:15:36,808
I've been wanting
to get out of the house.

325
00:15:42,439 --> 00:15:45,024
Yeah. We've got Hutten.
We'll be back in a couple hours.

326
00:15:47,402 --> 00:15:48,445
- Ready?
- One second.

327
00:15:51,364 --> 00:15:52,407
All right. Let's go.

328
00:15:56,453 --> 00:15:58,329
FBI! Hands where I can see them!

329
00:15:58,455 --> 00:16:00,039
Hey, take it easy!
I'm FBI, too!

330
00:16:00,165 --> 00:16:01,666
Kimball Cho.
This is Wayne Rigsby.

331
00:16:02,167 --> 00:16:03,209
It's cool.

332
00:16:04,252 --> 00:16:06,546
I'm Miller,
Houston bank robbery division.

333
00:16:07,213 --> 00:16:08,381
What the hell
are you doing with Hutten?

334
00:16:08,715 --> 00:16:09,841
We're taking him in
for questioning.

335
00:16:10,133 --> 00:16:11,176
No, you can't do that.

336
00:16:11,301 --> 00:16:12,469
- And why not?
- Why not?

337
00:16:12,886 --> 00:16:13,928
Because he works for me.

338
00:16:22,768 --> 00:16:24,979
Hutten's been working for my team
for the past year

339
00:16:25,104 --> 00:16:27,940
to take down bank-robbery rings.
That's why they let him out early.

340
00:16:28,232 --> 00:16:29,317
Did I not mention that?

341
00:16:29,734 --> 00:16:31,486
He's also a suspect
in two homicides.

342
00:16:32,528 --> 00:16:33,571
Homicides?

343
00:16:33,905 --> 00:16:34,947
You're kidding. When?

344
00:16:35,198 --> 00:16:37,074
Within the last two weeks
both in San Francisco.

345
00:16:37,742 --> 00:16:39,911
- Has Hutten been there recently?
- San Jose.

346
00:16:40,119 --> 00:16:41,996
We had an operation there.
Just got back yesterday.

347
00:16:42,121 --> 00:16:43,539
But you got the wrong guy.

348
00:16:43,706 --> 00:16:45,082
San Jose's an hour
from my house.

349
00:16:45,208 --> 00:16:46,250
- He could've done this.
- Yeah.

350
00:16:46,626 --> 00:16:48,794
No, see,
I think miller would've known.

351
00:16:49,795 --> 00:16:50,838
Do you see that dot?

352
00:16:51,797 --> 00:16:54,509
That's Hutten. He wears
an ankle-monitoring device.

353
00:16:54,675 --> 00:16:55,801
I know where he is at all times.

354
00:16:55,927 --> 00:16:57,887
That's how I knew he left the house,
for God's sake.

355
00:16:58,596 --> 00:16:59,639
He's not your killer.

356
00:16:59,764 --> 00:17:02,391
- We're done here, aren't we?
- Yeah. Yeah, we are.

357
00:17:03,142 --> 00:17:04,185
Best of luck, guys.

358
00:17:17,949 --> 00:17:19,534
- You from the FBI?
- Yes.

359
00:17:20,326 --> 00:17:22,161
Samuel Millman.
I own this place.

360
00:17:22,537 --> 00:17:24,497
- Welcome, welcome.
- Agent Kim Fischer.

361
00:17:25,122 --> 00:17:26,791
- Patrick Jane.
- Jane.

362
00:17:28,584 --> 00:17:29,710
Jason told me about you.

363
00:17:30,169 --> 00:17:33,214
Maybe you agree with what
these people did to my company.

364
00:17:34,006 --> 00:17:35,341
But come on.
I'll show you something there.

365
00:17:38,511 --> 00:17:40,054
"Stop fracking now!"

366
00:17:40,346 --> 00:17:41,764
"Stop fracking now!"

367
00:17:42,014 --> 00:17:43,474
"Stop fracking now!"

368
00:17:51,649 --> 00:17:55,278
They call themselves the cooperative
whatever the hell that means.

369
00:17:55,611 --> 00:17:57,488
The music is some kind
of calling card.

370
00:17:58,781 --> 00:18:00,908
You know, in my day, if you had
a problem with someone

371
00:18:01,409 --> 00:18:03,369
you would just tell them
face-to-face

372
00:18:03,578 --> 00:18:05,913
none of this
computer-hacking nonsense...

373
00:18:06,163 --> 00:18:08,291
Not that I pretend to understand
any of it.

374
00:18:08,833 --> 00:18:11,961
Are you looking into the possibility
that they killed that poor man, Becker?

375
00:18:12,420 --> 00:18:14,547
Your Mr. Kern asked
exactly the same thing.

376
00:18:14,922 --> 00:18:17,216
- It's a valid theory.
- Yeah, perhaps.

377
00:18:17,675 --> 00:18:20,136
But the cooperative
is an online activist group.

378
00:18:20,553 --> 00:18:21,804
Murder is a different thing.

379
00:18:23,264 --> 00:18:24,515
Well, look at that.

380
00:18:25,308 --> 00:18:27,852
You speak of the devil,
and he shall appear.

381
00:18:28,227 --> 00:18:30,354
Mr. Jane, Ms. Fischer.

382
00:18:30,521 --> 00:18:32,607
To what do we owe
the pleasure?

383
00:18:33,316 --> 00:18:35,568
Did either of you deal directly
with Dan Becker?

384
00:18:35,985 --> 00:18:37,653
Not me. In the last few years

385
00:18:37,778 --> 00:18:40,448
I've taken a step back
from the day-to-day operations.

386
00:18:40,740 --> 00:18:44,201
The company shifted its focus
to our fracking activities.

387
00:18:44,327 --> 00:18:46,662
That's my area of expertise.

388
00:18:46,829 --> 00:18:49,498
Oh, so you would've been the one
who ordered the assault on Becker.

389
00:18:51,459 --> 00:18:52,501
That's funny. Yeah.

390
00:18:53,210 --> 00:18:54,837
No, there have been
no assaults ordered

391
00:18:54,962 --> 00:18:56,589
or anything like that.

392
00:18:59,425 --> 00:19:01,677
You'd think a man in your position
would be a better liar.

393
00:19:02,178 --> 00:19:04,680
Mr. Jane, we're not an evil company.

394
00:19:05,097 --> 00:19:08,142
- You truly believe that, don't you?
- Of course I do!

395
00:19:08,351 --> 00:19:10,311
Look, we're the victims here.

396
00:19:13,147 --> 00:19:14,190
What the hell?

397
00:19:14,315 --> 00:19:15,358
What...

398
00:19:18,402 --> 00:19:19,445
What's going on here?

399
00:19:20,488 --> 00:19:21,614
- Jason?
- I don't know.

400
00:19:22,198 --> 00:19:23,491
"This is the cooperative."

401
00:19:24,742 --> 00:19:27,495
"Millman oil has committed
its latest crime..."

402
00:19:28,496 --> 00:19:31,415
"The murder of a man
named Dan Becker."

403
00:19:32,333 --> 00:19:33,918
"This will not go unpunished."

404
00:19:34,543 --> 00:19:36,504
"The cooperative
will be listening."

405
00:19:37,421 --> 00:19:39,340
"The cooperative
will be watching."

406
00:19:40,091 --> 00:19:42,968
"And we will not rest
until justice is served."

407
00:19:43,803 --> 00:19:45,054
Well, that was weird.

408
00:19:45,721 --> 00:19:48,432
And it's obvious they think Millman oil's
behind this but why?

409
00:19:48,683 --> 00:19:50,768
You said that the cooperative
hacked into their office.

410
00:19:50,976 --> 00:19:52,853
Maybe they found something
to connect them to the murder.

411
00:19:53,145 --> 00:19:54,480
So why not come to us with it?

412
00:19:54,605 --> 00:19:57,066
These kind of groups aren't
what you'd call "FBI friendly."

413
00:19:57,191 --> 00:19:58,901
We're investigating half of them
for cybercrimes.

414
00:19:59,235 --> 00:20:01,278
So how do we find out
what the cooperative knows?

415
00:20:01,487 --> 00:20:03,197
You could start
with whoever made that video.

416
00:20:03,698 --> 00:20:05,950
- Can you track him down, Wylie?
- Yeah, I can try.

417
00:20:06,158 --> 00:20:07,201
Let me know
if you find anything.

418
00:20:09,370 --> 00:20:11,038
Well, I heard one
of your suspects didn't pan out.

419
00:20:11,205 --> 00:20:12,498
Hutten? Big bust.

420
00:20:12,623 --> 00:20:14,083
We only have a few more names
on the list.

421
00:20:14,208 --> 00:20:15,251
We're running low.

422
00:20:15,584 --> 00:20:16,627
Lisbon.

423
00:20:17,962 --> 00:20:19,088
I need to see you
in my office.

424
00:20:25,970 --> 00:20:27,847
Agent Lisbon, Laura Dubin...

425
00:20:30,599 --> 00:20:31,934
Attorney for Richard Haibach.

426
00:20:36,355 --> 00:20:39,817
Mr. Haibach is in Denver
but I'm going to conference him in.

427
00:20:41,152 --> 00:20:44,655
Hello, Richard. I'm here
with agents Abbott and Lisbon.

428
00:20:44,822 --> 00:20:47,616
- "You damn people are..."
- One moment, Richard, please.

429
00:20:49,994 --> 00:20:54,790
Agent Abbott, did you know agent Lisbon
contacted Mr. Haibach's employer

430
00:20:54,915 --> 00:20:58,377
to ask about his whereabouts
During Osvaldo Ardiles' murder?

431
00:20:58,669 --> 00:20:59,837
I was not aware of that.

432
00:21:00,296 --> 00:21:01,338
For the record

433
00:21:01,464 --> 00:21:04,300
Mr. Haibach was at a dinner
with two coworkers.

434
00:21:04,425 --> 00:21:08,763
"I want to know what justification you have
for endangering my career!"

435
00:21:08,888 --> 00:21:12,433
This is an active investigation.
I'm not at liberty to say more.

436
00:21:12,641 --> 00:21:14,101
Which means you have nothing to say.

437
00:21:14,477 --> 00:21:15,519
Agent Abbott

438
00:21:15,644 --> 00:21:18,689
my client has suffered enough
at the hands of agent Lisbon.

439
00:21:19,523 --> 00:21:22,943
If you continue to do this
we will have no choice but to sue.

440
00:21:23,819 --> 00:21:24,945
That won't be necessary.

441
00:21:25,488 --> 00:21:26,530
Good.

442
00:21:27,364 --> 00:21:30,534
I may consider the investigation
into Mr. Haibach closed?

443
00:21:31,410 --> 00:21:32,453
You may.

444
00:21:32,620 --> 00:21:36,165
"I want an apology from her
from agent Lisbon's lips!"

445
00:21:48,844 --> 00:21:52,807
Mr. Haibach, I am very sorry
if I've caused you any inconvenience.

446
00:21:52,932 --> 00:21:54,892
- It won't happen again.
- "It better not!"

447
00:21:59,063 --> 00:22:00,105
Agents.

448
00:22:05,861 --> 00:22:08,405
- Please, sir, you can't drop this.
- We're not.

449
00:22:09,824 --> 00:22:12,034
There's something off
about that man.

450
00:22:12,535 --> 00:22:14,328
I want you to put a surveillance
team together

451
00:22:14,870 --> 00:22:16,914
but just give it 24 hours
before you do.

452
00:22:17,623 --> 00:22:19,208
I want him to think
that he's off the hook.

453
00:22:19,750 --> 00:22:20,793
Yes, sir.

454
00:22:23,587 --> 00:22:25,005
Okay, buddy.
I'll see you soon.

455
00:22:25,756 --> 00:22:26,799
I love you.

456
00:22:30,010 --> 00:22:31,595
- How's Ben?
- He's fine.

457
00:22:32,221 --> 00:22:33,639
Please tell me
you have some good news.

458
00:22:33,764 --> 00:22:34,807
Wish I did.

459
00:22:35,015 --> 00:22:36,058
Volker's in federal lockup

460
00:22:36,183 --> 00:22:39,061
so I had the prison check his
communications for the last six months.

461
00:22:39,395 --> 00:22:40,437
There's nothing there.

462
00:22:40,563 --> 00:22:41,814
Anything on the other
seven suspects?

463
00:22:42,106 --> 00:22:43,774
A few leads.
No breakthroughs.

464
00:22:44,275 --> 00:22:47,778
Which leaves us with Haibach
who we have nothing on.

465
00:22:48,863 --> 00:22:50,573
Take a break.
Get something to eat.

466
00:22:51,949 --> 00:22:53,492
Yeah. Okay. Thanks.

467
00:22:57,288 --> 00:22:58,330
Fudge!

468
00:22:59,665 --> 00:23:01,584
Of course he used an anonymizing
proxy server.

469
00:23:01,709 --> 00:23:03,210
Why would you want
to make my life easy?

470
00:23:04,712 --> 00:23:05,963
Was I talking out loud?

471
00:23:06,505 --> 00:23:07,965
No problem.
I'm Grace.

472
00:23:08,716 --> 00:23:12,219
I'm Wylie. Sorry if I disturbed you.
Excuse me.

473
00:23:14,388 --> 00:23:15,431
Want some help?

474
00:23:15,681 --> 00:23:18,559
Ever try cracking a daisy-chained
anonymizing proxy server?

475
00:23:18,726 --> 00:23:19,977
Sure. What's going on?

476
00:23:22,229 --> 00:23:25,524
Well, uh, a cooperative member
posted a video earlier today.

477
00:23:25,691 --> 00:23:27,276
I'm looking for the upload location.

478
00:23:27,526 --> 00:23:30,237
Problem is, he routed it
through 70 different servers.

479
00:23:30,487 --> 00:23:33,115
Well, a lot of times these guys use public
computers to avoid detection.

480
00:23:33,324 --> 00:23:35,242
I checked that...
20 of the servers are public locations.

481
00:23:35,409 --> 00:23:37,202
Did you unlock
the video's file attributes?

482
00:23:37,828 --> 00:23:40,080
I know some good
forensic-decryption software.

483
00:23:41,123 --> 00:23:43,292
- Dorsey, maybe?
- McClaren. It's faster.

484
00:23:50,049 --> 00:23:51,091
Here we go...

485
00:23:51,467 --> 00:23:54,929
He uploaded the video today
at about 5:00 p.m.

486
00:23:57,723 --> 00:24:00,059
Interesting.
He posted dozens of videos

487
00:24:00,184 --> 00:24:02,436
all of them uploaded between
5:00 p.m. and midnight.

488
00:24:02,770 --> 00:24:04,355
Any of the public locations
open late?

489
00:24:08,817 --> 00:24:11,654
Wylie says our guy's been
uploading videos from this lab

490
00:24:11,779 --> 00:24:14,198
almost every night
between 5:00 and midnight.

491
00:24:14,782 --> 00:24:16,408
It's 9:00 now, so he may be here.

492
00:24:18,494 --> 00:24:19,662
Yeah.

493
00:24:19,995 --> 00:24:21,038
But it could be anyone.

494
00:24:25,876 --> 00:24:26,919
It's him.

495
00:24:29,213 --> 00:24:30,255
Are you sure?

496
00:24:30,381 --> 00:24:31,882
Yeah, look at his eyes...
all squirrelly.

497
00:24:32,549 --> 00:24:33,592
Don't you think?

498
00:24:38,597 --> 00:24:39,640
Excuse me, sir?

499
00:24:41,016 --> 00:24:43,060
FBI. I need to ask you
a few questions, please.

500
00:24:44,853 --> 00:24:45,896
Fischer.

501
00:24:46,355 --> 00:24:47,731
I lied. It's him.

502
00:24:50,317 --> 00:24:51,360
Sir...

503
00:24:52,069 --> 00:24:54,321
Wa... FBI. Stop!

504
00:24:56,281 --> 00:24:57,950
- Hey, hey!
- What are you doing?!

505
00:24:58,075 --> 00:24:59,284
- Sorry.
- Don't do that!

506
00:24:59,910 --> 00:25:02,663
- Sorry. Shh.
- Leave him alone!

507
00:25:17,092 --> 00:25:18,135
Ethan Zorn.

508
00:25:19,386 --> 00:25:22,014
Enrolled at U.T.
at the age of 14

509
00:25:22,723 --> 00:25:24,266
dropped out
after one semester.

510
00:25:25,892 --> 00:25:28,353
Arrested a year later
for hacking into Nasa.

511
00:25:28,645 --> 00:25:30,105
I assume
you have a point.

512
00:25:30,522 --> 00:25:34,234
I don't think that you are a run-of-the-mill
cooperative type.

513
00:25:35,527 --> 00:25:37,154
If I had to take a guess...

514
00:25:39,865 --> 00:25:42,409
I would say that you are
the one in charge.

515
00:25:45,537 --> 00:25:49,207
You believe that Millman oil
is responsible for Dan Becker's death.

516
00:25:50,167 --> 00:25:53,003
- Why?
- Well, call it a hunch.

517
00:25:53,295 --> 00:25:55,172
I don't think you understand,
Mr. Zorn.

518
00:25:55,922 --> 00:25:58,592
The federal government
doesn't treat cybercrimes lightly.

519
00:25:59,926 --> 00:26:05,807
Conspiracy to engage in hacking
carries a 10-year sentence.

520
00:26:07,225 --> 00:26:09,770
Now, you may be tough
behind a computer

521
00:26:10,687 --> 00:26:11,855
but in a cellblock...

522
00:26:21,948 --> 00:26:24,743
The... the cooperative
has an e-mail account

523
00:26:24,868 --> 00:26:26,703
where whistle-blowers
can get in touch.

524
00:26:27,704 --> 00:26:30,582
Dan sent a note regarding
his troubled lawsuit.

525
00:26:30,707 --> 00:26:33,085
- He wanted my help.
- Your help for what?

526
00:26:34,086 --> 00:26:36,254
He heard a rumor
that an executive at Millman oil

527
00:26:36,379 --> 00:26:39,758
kept a black book of names...
all the people he'd paid off.

528
00:26:40,801 --> 00:26:41,843
Dan wanted that book.

529
00:26:42,219 --> 00:26:43,970
An executive...
Jason Kern.

530
00:26:44,179 --> 00:26:45,222
Exactly.

531
00:26:45,680 --> 00:26:49,267
So, together, Dan and I hatched a plan.
I would organize a protest

532
00:26:49,392 --> 00:26:50,602
at the front of the building.

533
00:26:51,186 --> 00:26:52,562
That would distract security

534
00:26:52,687 --> 00:26:55,357
and Dan would be able to break
into kern's office in the back.

535
00:26:55,857 --> 00:26:58,652
We staged it on a Sunday
so nobody would be in the office.

536
00:26:58,777 --> 00:27:01,404
Okay, what about the alarms,
the locks, the cameras?

537
00:27:02,697 --> 00:27:04,991
A joke for someone
with my skills.

538
00:27:06,827 --> 00:27:08,370
I hacked the alarm systems,

539
00:27:08,620 --> 00:27:13,083
and I gave Dan a list of Jason kern's
possible pass-code combinations

540
00:27:13,416 --> 00:27:14,459
if needed.

541
00:27:15,043 --> 00:27:17,838
Most people are very predictable
in their choices.

542
00:27:19,881 --> 00:27:22,509
Well, something didn't go
according to plan.

543
00:27:22,801 --> 00:27:25,011
Uh, either Dan
was discovered in the act

544
00:27:25,137 --> 00:27:26,721
or he was tracked down
afterwards.

545
00:27:27,305 --> 00:27:30,642
All I know for sure is that I never heard
from him again.

546
00:27:33,228 --> 00:27:34,813
The tanker would then cost
a million bucks.

547
00:27:35,063 --> 00:27:36,398
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

548
00:27:36,523 --> 00:27:38,733
- Excuse me?
- Your secretary's out to lunch.

549
00:27:38,859 --> 00:27:40,944
Hope you don't mind
we let ourselves in.

550
00:27:41,111 --> 00:27:42,154
What are you doing here?

551
00:27:42,279 --> 00:27:45,031
Why didn't you tell us that Dan Becker
broke into your office?

552
00:27:45,240 --> 00:27:48,076
- Because he didn't.
- Oh, yes, he did.

553
00:27:48,660 --> 00:27:53,290
And he searched the place
until he found your safe.

554
00:27:55,292 --> 00:27:56,334
Safe.

555
00:27:58,420 --> 00:28:01,214
And he opened it and he stole
your little black book of names.

556
00:28:01,339 --> 00:28:03,008
Jason, what are they talking about?

557
00:28:03,508 --> 00:28:06,678
Mr. Kern here has been bribing
residents and city officials

558
00:28:06,803 --> 00:28:08,930
to keep them from testifying
against your company.

559
00:28:09,264 --> 00:28:10,307
This is ridiculous.

560
00:28:10,891 --> 00:28:12,809
All right, I don't have
a black book, and even if I did

561
00:28:12,934 --> 00:28:15,312
how did Becker get into my safe
without a combination?

562
00:28:15,478 --> 00:28:16,521
Well, he did.

563
00:28:16,646 --> 00:28:18,607
You obviously had a very
simple code... your birth date?

564
00:28:19,024 --> 00:28:20,066
- How d...
- Yes.

565
00:28:20,859 --> 00:28:23,445
Was anything else taken,
aside from the book?

566
00:28:23,570 --> 00:28:26,323
- No.
- That was a yes.

567
00:28:26,448 --> 00:28:29,993
- The book and what else?
- Nothing was taken, all right?

568
00:28:30,118 --> 00:28:31,828
No black book, no nothing!

569
00:28:31,953 --> 00:28:34,372
All right, look, you know what?
I'm sorry, but you people need to leave.

570
00:28:34,539 --> 00:28:36,374
We just have a few more questions.

571
00:28:36,499 --> 00:28:38,251
Then I'm gonna have
to bring in our lawyers.

572
00:28:38,501 --> 00:28:39,544
Exactly.

573
00:28:39,669 --> 00:28:41,129
- Oh, this is interesting.
- What?

574
00:28:41,296 --> 00:28:42,672
Look at the indentation
on the carpet.

575
00:28:42,797 --> 00:28:44,299
Someone has moved
this file-cabinet thing

576
00:28:44,507 --> 00:28:46,218
when they were searching
for the safe.

577
00:28:46,676 --> 00:28:48,220
How many times
do I have to tell you?

578
00:28:48,345 --> 00:28:49,596
There was no break-in!

579
00:28:54,351 --> 00:28:55,393
We're done here.

580
00:29:03,735 --> 00:29:05,237
So, you think Dan
took something else.

581
00:29:05,820 --> 00:29:06,947
- Like what?
- I don't know.

582
00:29:07,155 --> 00:29:08,949
Money, maybe.
Kern would need a slush fund.

583
00:29:09,157 --> 00:29:10,700
I mean, he's not paying
people off with checks.

584
00:29:11,159 --> 00:29:12,869
I need to make a stop
on the way back.

585
00:29:13,078 --> 00:29:15,205
- Where are we going?
- Talk to Dan's friends.

586
00:29:15,580 --> 00:29:18,166
- Why?
- Patience.

587
00:29:21,962 --> 00:29:23,838
Ah, everyone is here.

588
00:29:23,964 --> 00:29:25,298
Please, come on in.
Take a seat.

589
00:29:25,966 --> 00:29:27,008
Thank you.

590
00:29:27,133 --> 00:29:29,386
There's been a very interesting
development in the case.

591
00:29:30,053 --> 00:29:32,389
I'm gonna need you all to pay
very careful attention.

592
00:29:33,431 --> 00:29:34,808
Agent Fischer, if you would.

593
00:29:35,558 --> 00:29:36,601
Thank you.

594
00:29:37,394 --> 00:29:40,272
Now, does this song
mean something to any of you?

595
00:29:43,024 --> 00:29:46,069
"London bridge is falling down"?
It's a nursery rhyme, isn't it?

596
00:29:46,403 --> 00:29:47,445
Anything else?

597
00:29:48,071 --> 00:29:49,114
Bryce?

598
00:29:50,865 --> 00:29:51,908
Susan?

599
00:29:53,743 --> 00:29:54,786
Molly?

600
00:29:56,288 --> 00:29:57,497
This is why you brought us here?

601
00:30:00,250 --> 00:30:01,293
Thank you for your time.

602
00:30:02,002 --> 00:30:03,128
Sorry for the inconvenience.

603
00:30:11,928 --> 00:30:12,971
Are you gonna tell me
what this is about?

604
00:30:13,096 --> 00:30:14,139
Nope.

605
00:30:21,438 --> 00:30:22,564
I want to talk to Hutten again.

606
00:30:23,189 --> 00:30:24,566
- No.
- Just hear me out.

607
00:30:24,941 --> 00:30:26,234
He's wearing a monitoring device.

608
00:30:26,359 --> 00:30:27,527
I checked in with miller's unit

609
00:30:27,694 --> 00:30:29,696
and they say that Hutten hasn't
left the house in 14 hours.

610
00:30:29,821 --> 00:30:31,448
- And...
- That doesn't seem strange at all?

611
00:30:32,365 --> 00:30:33,408
Not necessarily.

612
00:30:33,533 --> 00:30:35,076
I also tried to call Hutten.
No answer.

613
00:30:36,077 --> 00:30:38,913
Look, maybe it's nothing
but we don't have any other leads.

614
00:30:39,164 --> 00:30:40,206
What do we have to lose?

615
00:30:42,792 --> 00:30:44,669
Just so you know,
this is a waste of time.

616
00:30:55,764 --> 00:30:56,806
You sure Hutten's home?

617
00:31:01,936 --> 00:31:03,646
He's here.
He's probably just sleeping.

618
00:31:04,981 --> 00:31:06,399
John, open up!

619
00:31:08,193 --> 00:31:10,070
- You got a key?
- No, just wait.

620
00:31:10,862 --> 00:31:11,905
Excuse me.

621
00:31:13,073 --> 00:31:14,616
- What the hell are you doing?!
- Sorry.

622
00:31:16,910 --> 00:31:17,952
You can't go in there!

623
00:31:24,918 --> 00:31:25,960
He's not upstairs.

624
00:31:27,796 --> 00:31:28,838
I'll call in a bolo.

625
00:31:31,174 --> 00:31:32,217
This doesn't make any sense.

626
00:31:32,342 --> 00:31:33,593
His monitor says that he's right here.

627
00:31:37,889 --> 00:31:38,932
Yeah.

628
00:31:41,393 --> 00:31:42,519
'Cause he modified his cellphone

629
00:31:42,644 --> 00:31:44,479
to give out the same signal
as the ankle monitor.

630
00:31:45,146 --> 00:31:47,524
You leave it on, everybody
thinks he's still home.

631
00:31:50,944 --> 00:31:53,154
Yep. I'm guessing he's gone.

632
00:31:59,409 --> 00:32:00,451
Thanks, Cho. Got it.

633
00:32:00,618 --> 00:32:02,078
I just got off the phone
with Hutten's wife.

634
00:32:02,203 --> 00:32:03,663
She's been in San Diego
for two days

635
00:32:03,788 --> 00:32:05,665
and has no idea
where her husband is.

636
00:32:06,374 --> 00:32:07,417
Any luck?

637
00:32:07,542 --> 00:32:08,584
Cho just got a hit
on the bolo.

638
00:32:08,709 --> 00:32:10,837
A convenience-store tenant
saw Hutten 20 minutes ago.

639
00:32:11,087 --> 00:32:12,922
He left traveling west
in a blue jeep.

640
00:32:14,882 --> 00:32:17,009
Okay. I've got the nearest
traffic-light camera.

641
00:32:17,301 --> 00:32:18,344
There it is.

642
00:32:20,179 --> 00:32:21,472
He turned left on Hampton.

643
00:32:21,681 --> 00:32:23,641
I'll start pulling more footage.
Maybe I can track where he's headed.

644
00:32:23,850 --> 00:32:24,892
All righty.

645
00:32:25,435 --> 00:32:28,146
Wylie. You got five minutes
to help catch a killer?

646
00:32:29,147 --> 00:32:31,441
- Yes. Absolutely.
- Good.

647
00:32:31,774 --> 00:32:34,152
How much can you really learn
about someone on the internet...

648
00:32:34,277 --> 00:32:37,238
- I mean, intimate details?
- You'd be surprised.

649
00:32:37,405 --> 00:32:39,365
Of course, if you have a court order
for phone records

650
00:32:39,490 --> 00:32:41,492
and credit-card transactions
you can find a lot more.

651
00:32:43,619 --> 00:32:47,707
- Do we have a court order?
- Would I be asking if we didn't?

652
00:32:53,004 --> 00:32:54,755
"Everything's going
according to plan."

653
00:32:54,922 --> 00:32:57,049
"Agent Collins is at his house,
siphoning his gas."

654
00:33:25,745 --> 00:33:27,455
"Okay, he's headed out now."

655
00:33:29,207 --> 00:33:31,667
All right. It's working.
He pulled into the gas station.

656
00:33:32,335 --> 00:33:34,795
- Are we all set on your end?
- "All set. Good to go."

657
00:33:35,213 --> 00:33:36,255
All right.

658
00:34:09,539 --> 00:34:11,249
Hey, man, what's wrong
with these radios, huh?

659
00:34:11,415 --> 00:34:12,833
Don't know. Just came on.

660
00:34:17,004 --> 00:34:18,047
"Hello, Bryce."

661
00:34:22,635 --> 00:34:25,096
"Yeah, you.
I'm talking to you, Bryce."

662
00:34:26,347 --> 00:34:27,390
Hello?

663
00:34:28,432 --> 00:34:29,642
"This is the cooperative."

664
00:34:29,767 --> 00:34:31,269
"Did you really think you were
gonna get away with it?"

665
00:34:34,814 --> 00:34:35,856
What?

666
00:34:37,316 --> 00:34:38,359
What the hell's going on?

667
00:34:39,610 --> 00:34:42,071
"Oh, come on.
We know you killed Dan Becker."

668
00:34:46,534 --> 00:34:47,660
No, I did not.

669
00:34:48,411 --> 00:34:49,495
I would never hurt Dan.

670
00:34:51,080 --> 00:34:54,083
"We know you did it,
and now we know why."

671
00:34:54,834 --> 00:34:56,669
"You helped Dan break into
Jason Kern's office"

672
00:34:56,877 --> 00:34:58,671
"but you didn't just find
the black book, did you?"

673
00:34:58,796 --> 00:35:00,881
"No, you wouldn't murder
Dan for that."

674
00:35:01,007 --> 00:35:02,300
"You found kern's slush fund."

675
00:35:02,883 --> 00:35:05,344
"You killed Dan so you could have
the money for yourself."

676
00:35:08,598 --> 00:35:09,640
No, I didn't.

677
00:35:10,516 --> 00:35:13,269
Don't worry.
We're not turning you in.

678
00:35:14,270 --> 00:35:16,772
The cooperative
makes its own justice.

679
00:35:17,732 --> 00:35:20,067
"You're not gonna get away
with this, Bryce."

680
00:35:21,736 --> 00:35:22,778
What are you gonna do?

681
00:35:22,903 --> 00:35:25,114
Well, we know where you hid
the money, and we took it.

682
00:35:25,615 --> 00:35:28,367
- You couldn't possibly know that.
- We know everything, Bryce.

683
00:35:29,577 --> 00:35:30,620
Don't believe us?

684
00:35:31,203 --> 00:35:33,164
Today for lunch you ordered
a ham and cheese

685
00:35:33,289 --> 00:35:34,457
with chips and a drink.

686
00:35:34,749 --> 00:35:36,667
"Last week,
you saw a dermatologist"

687
00:35:36,792 --> 00:35:38,961
"for a reoccurring case
of athlete's foot."

688
00:35:39,712 --> 00:35:41,297
Two months ago,
you were fired

689
00:35:41,422 --> 00:35:44,550
after a coworker accused you
of sexual harassment.

690
00:35:45,134 --> 00:35:46,177
Now do you believe me?

691
00:35:46,469 --> 00:35:47,720
How could you
possibly know that?

692
00:35:48,012 --> 00:35:49,305
I told you...
we know everything

693
00:35:49,430 --> 00:35:51,891
and we made sure you wouldn't
profit from Dan Becker's death.

694
00:35:52,516 --> 00:35:53,559
Goodbye, Bryce.

695
00:35:55,478 --> 00:35:56,520
Hello?

696
00:36:36,602 --> 00:36:37,687
What do you got there, Bryce?

697
00:36:44,568 --> 00:36:46,153
Now, we know you helped Dan.

698
00:36:46,987 --> 00:36:48,030
He had a bad back.

699
00:36:48,823 --> 00:36:50,658
He couldn't have moved
that file cabinet by himself.

700
00:36:53,994 --> 00:36:55,621
Anybody could've helped him.

701
00:36:56,706 --> 00:36:57,748
Why me?

702
00:36:57,873 --> 00:36:58,916
Jane could tell you were
the only one

703
00:36:59,083 --> 00:37:01,544
who knew what "London bridge
is falling down" really meant.

704
00:37:02,336 --> 00:37:04,922
You heard the cooperative playing it
during the break-in, didn't you?

705
00:37:10,261 --> 00:37:11,303
Yeah, I helped Dan.

706
00:37:12,513 --> 00:37:14,098
He wanted backup
in case something went wrong.

707
00:37:16,016 --> 00:37:17,268
Found the safe in Kern's office.

708
00:37:17,518 --> 00:37:20,563
Cash was in it,
along with the black book.

709
00:37:23,232 --> 00:37:25,025
$100,000, man...

710
00:37:26,527 --> 00:37:28,404
- But Dan didn't want the money?
- No.

711
00:37:29,655 --> 00:37:30,698
Thought it would make him a thief.

712
00:37:32,283 --> 00:37:33,909
But I didn't.
I needed that money.

713
00:37:34,952 --> 00:37:35,995
I deserved that money.

714
00:37:36,912 --> 00:37:38,831
Everybody else is getting rich, huh?
Why couldn't I have a little?

715
00:37:39,874 --> 00:37:41,542
"Figured what Dan didn't know
wouldn't hurt him."

716
00:37:41,876 --> 00:37:42,918
Let's get out of here.

717
00:37:43,419 --> 00:37:44,795
"Stop fracking now!"

718
00:37:45,004 --> 00:37:48,090
"Stop fracking now!"

719
00:37:48,215 --> 00:37:49,300
So, what went wrong?

720
00:37:53,512 --> 00:37:54,555
Bad luck, I guess.

721
00:38:00,728 --> 00:38:01,771
Thanks, man.

722
00:38:02,980 --> 00:38:04,023
Nice work.

723
00:38:08,569 --> 00:38:09,987
- What the hell is this?!
- Hey, hey, wait a minute, Dan!

724
00:38:10,279 --> 00:38:12,656
Let me explain!
Let me explain!

725
00:38:12,990 --> 00:38:14,033
We're not keeping the money.

726
00:38:14,366 --> 00:38:15,576
That's not what
we were supposed to do.

727
00:38:15,701 --> 00:38:16,786
We deserve it, Dan!

728
00:38:16,911 --> 00:38:18,788
They are profiting
off of our land!

729
00:38:18,913 --> 00:38:21,081
Doesn't make it right.
I'm giving it back.

730
00:38:21,207 --> 00:38:22,249
The hell you are.

731
00:38:23,667 --> 00:38:24,710
Hey.

732
00:38:33,928 --> 00:38:35,221
"Ah, he just wasn't breathing."

733
00:38:36,889 --> 00:38:37,932
"And, uh..."

734
00:38:39,809 --> 00:38:40,851
I didn't know what to do.

735
00:38:44,438 --> 00:38:46,857
So you dumped the body
on Millman oil property.

736
00:38:47,024 --> 00:38:49,235
I figured if anybody deserved
to get in trouble, it was them.

737
00:38:54,365 --> 00:38:56,200
But I never meant
to hurt Dan.

738
00:39:02,248 --> 00:39:03,415
Mr. Kern.

739
00:39:05,501 --> 00:39:07,962
It's a very detailed book.
Thank you for this.

740
00:39:09,421 --> 00:39:10,631
We have a lot to talk about, don't we?

741
00:39:10,756 --> 00:39:13,467
Oh, this'll be tied up in court
for years, and you know it.

742
00:39:14,510 --> 00:39:15,970
We have some
very good lawyers.

743
00:39:16,470 --> 00:39:19,390
They won't be working for you.
You're fired.

744
00:39:19,515 --> 00:39:21,100
Samuel, we can beat this.

745
00:39:21,225 --> 00:39:23,477
I'm done with your lies, Jason.

746
00:39:24,395 --> 00:39:26,397
- Get him out of my sight.
- Samuel, wait.

747
00:39:27,106 --> 00:39:28,148
Samuel!

748
00:39:38,409 --> 00:39:40,995
Hold on. Back up.
Blue jeep.

749
00:39:55,342 --> 00:39:56,385
Hey.

750
00:39:58,637 --> 00:39:59,680
FBI, ma'am.

751
00:40:00,180 --> 00:40:01,223
All right.

752
00:40:01,640 --> 00:40:02,683
Aw, man.

753
00:40:03,309 --> 00:40:05,144
Any chance you guys
can come back in a half an hour?

754
00:40:06,186 --> 00:40:07,396
He's been hacking
the ankle monitor

755
00:40:07,521 --> 00:40:09,356
so he can bang his girlfriend
without his wife knowing.

756
00:40:09,565 --> 00:40:12,109
the mistress says she visited Hutten
last Thursday in San Jose

757
00:40:12,234 --> 00:40:13,277
the same day Ardiles died.

758
00:40:13,944 --> 00:40:15,321
They were in a hotel
the whole time.

759
00:40:15,446 --> 00:40:17,406
It checks out.
Hutten's not our guy.

760
00:40:17,990 --> 00:40:19,241
So, where are we
on Haibach?

761
00:40:19,575 --> 00:40:21,660
His surveillance detail should be up.
Let me call.

762
00:40:26,248 --> 00:40:27,625
- "Hello?"
- Agent burns, hi.

763
00:40:27,875 --> 00:40:29,960
This is agent Lisbon in Austin.

764
00:40:30,419 --> 00:40:33,339
- Anything to report?
- "No. We got nothing."

765
00:40:33,464 --> 00:40:34,548
"Haven't seen the guy all night."

766
00:40:34,798 --> 00:40:37,843
All right, well, keep me updated.
We got nothing.

767
00:40:40,679 --> 00:40:42,348
- I could use a drink.
- I'm in.

768
00:40:43,098 --> 00:40:44,808
- We should grab Jane.
- Count me out.

769
00:40:45,351 --> 00:40:46,393
- You sure?
- Yeah, I'm beat.

770
00:40:46,644 --> 00:40:47,686
I'll see you guys tomorrow.

771
00:40:47,811 --> 00:40:48,979
- All right, Lisbon.
- Good night. Okay.

772
00:41:01,700 --> 00:41:03,118
Hey. I fell asleep.

773
00:41:03,327 --> 00:41:04,912
Oh, I'm sorry.
I just wanted to check in.

774
00:41:05,037 --> 00:41:06,205
I'm out having
a drink with the guys.

775
00:41:08,082 --> 00:41:10,960
- What happened with Hutten?
- It's not him. I'll explain later.

776
00:41:11,669 --> 00:41:12,711
You go back to bed.

777
00:41:12,836 --> 00:41:14,630
- You having fun?
- Yeah. Yeah, I am.

778
00:41:16,215 --> 00:41:18,384
- I'm glad. It's good for you.
- Thank you.

779
00:41:19,301 --> 00:41:20,594
I love you, babe.
You know that, right?

780
00:41:21,387 --> 00:41:22,429
I love you, too.

781
00:41:22,596 --> 00:41:25,265
- Okay, I'll see you soon.
- Good night.


